[STEP 1] Recruitment
Group 1 -  Initial Assessment Team (IAT)
 Member 1 (Neonatologist (Lead)): Specializes in the care of newborns, particularly sick or premature infants. This member leads the group due to their expertise in managing acute conditions in neonates and the immediate assessment of respiratory distress and cyanosis.
 Member 2 (Pediatric Cardiologist): Provides specialized knowledge regarding congenital heart defects, assessing the newborn’s cardiac status, and interpreting ultrasound or echocardiogram findings to identify congenital anomalies.
 Member 3 (Pediatric Surgeon): Responsible for performing the atrial septostomy and any necessary surgical interventions to alleviate the newborn's condition. They collaborate closely with the cardiologist to ensure swift and effective treatment.

Group 2 -  Diagnostic Evidence Team (DET)
 Member 1 (Pediatric Radiologist (Lead)): Interprets imaging studies such as chest X
 Member 2 (Pediatric Intensivist): Focuses on managing the care of the critically ill newborn in the intensive care unit, ensuring effective monitoring and management of respiratory failure alongside diagnostic assessments.
 Member 3 (Clinical Geneticist): Evaluates for potential genetic syndromes that may contribute to congenital heart defects, including transposition of the great vessels, and advises on any relevant genetic testing.

Group 3 -  Final Review and Decision Team (FRDT)
 Member 1 (Senior Pediatric Cardiologist (Lead)): Provides overarching expertise in congenital heart disease, leading the group in making informed decisions on the definitive surgical approach based on all gathered information.
 Member 2 (Multidisciplinary Coordinator): Integrates the findings and recommendations from the IAT and DET to formulate a cohesive treatment plan that takes into account the newborn’s immediate needs and possible long
 Member 3 (Pediatric Anesthesiologist): Assesses the anesthetic risks associated with the surgery, planning for the needs of a critically ill neonate and coordinating with the surgical team to ensure safe anesthesia care during the atrial septostomy.


